---
import Legal from "../../layouts/Legal.astro";
---

<Legal
  noindex
  title="Privacy Policy — Saverr"
  description="How Saverr (com.propertysaver.app) handles your data: everything you save stays on your device. No accounts, no servers, no analytics, no ads and no network requests."
>
  <p class="app">Saverr</p>
  <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="dates">
    Effective date: 30 October 2026<br />
    Applies to the Saverr app (com.propertysaver.app)
  </p>

  <div class="summary">
    <h2>Summary</h2>
    <p>
      Everything you save stays on your device. There are no accounts, no
      servers, no analytics and no ads — and the app makes no network requests at
      all. It works offline. Tapping <strong>&ldquo;Open listing&rdquo;</strong> hands
      the link to your browser; that site&rsquo;s own privacy policy then applies.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="stores">
    <h2><span class="n">1.</span>What the app stores</h2>
    <p>On your device, in the app&rsquo;s private storage:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        property links you save, plus the details you enter (address, price,
        rooms, type, status, notes);
      </li>
      <li>app preferences, such as your filters and appearance settings.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This data does not leave the device unless you export it. Your
      phone&rsquo;s own backup (iCloud, Google) may include it depending on your
      device settings — Saverr itself never uploads anything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="collect">
    <h2><span class="n">2.</span>What we collect</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Nothing.</strong> There is no backend to receive it. We cannot see
      your shortlist, your notes, or how you use the app.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="backups">
    <h2><span class="n">3.</span>Backups you create</h2>
    <p>
      The backup screen can export your shortlist to a JSON file. That file is
      created on your device and handed to whatever destination you choose
      (Files, email, a cloud drive). It contains your listing links, details and
      notes — treat it as personal information, because it is.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="changes">
    <h2><span class="n">4.</span>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the effective date above is updated and the new
      version ships with the app.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="note">
    Listing names and prices shown in the app are entered by you, or copied by
    you from listing sites.
  </p>
</Legal>
